// Method Overriding ->
    Methods having same name , same return type , same parameter but perform different tasks
    and are defined in different classes .

    eg .   class Animal {
               void eat() {
                   System.out.println("Animal is eating");
               }
           }

           class Dog extends Animal {
               void eat() {
                   System.out.println("Dog is eating");
               }
           }

// Polymorphism ->
    It is an important concept of OOPs & defined as the ability of a message to be
    displayed in more than one form

    eg -> A person at the same time can have different characteristics like a man , at same time he is
    the father , a husband , a employee . so the same person for this different behaviour in different
    situations . This is called polymorphism .

// Types of polymorphism - >

    1. Compile Time Polymorphism -> This type of polymorphism can be achieved through method overloading
    2. Run Time Polymorphism -> This type of polymorphism can be achieved through method overriding


// Data Abstraction ->
    It is an important concept of OOPs . It refers to provide only essential information about the data to
    the outside world , Hiding the background details for implementation .

   - Real life example -> A man is driving a car , The man only knows that pressing the accelerator will increase
    the speed of the car or applying the brakes will stop the car , but he does not know about how on pressing
    accelerator the speed is actually increasing , he does not know about the inner mechanism of the car .


// Achieving Abstraction : Abstraction Classes
    An abstract class is a class that cannot be instantiated directly (you can't create  an object of it using
    new ) - it's meant only to be extended by subclasses . It can contain :

    Abstract methods - declared but with no body ; subclasses must provide the implementation
    Regular (concrete) methods - with a full body , inherited as-is .

    abstract class Shape {
        String color;
        // Abstract method - no body , just a signature
        abstract double calculateArea();

        // Regular (concrete) method - has a body
        void displayColor(){
            sout("color" +color);
        }
    }

    class Circle extends Shape {
        double radius ;
        Circle(double radius , String color){
            this.radius = radius;
            this.color = color;
        }
        @override
        double calculateArea(){
            return Math.PI * radius * radius ;
        }
    }
    Class Rectangle extends Shape {
        double length , width ;
        Rectangle(double length , double width, String color ){
            this.length = length ;
            this.width = width ;
            this.color = color;
        }

        @override
        double calculateArea(){
            return length * width;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args){
        // Shape s = new Shape();  // Error cannot instatiate an abstract class

        Shape c = new Circle(5,"red");
        Shape r = new Rectangle(4,6,"Blue");

        c.displayColor();
        sout("Area" + c.calculateArea());

        r.displayColor();
        sout("Area" + r.calculateArea());
    }


// Interface
    An interface is a blueprint that specifies what a class should do but not how it should do .

    Interface Syntax

    interface Drawable {
        void draw();  // implicitly public and abstract - no body allowed here
    }
    class Circle implements Drawable {
        @override
        public void draw(){
            sout("Drawing a circle");
        }
    }
    class Square implements Drawable {
        @override
        public void draw(){
            sout("Drawing square");
        }
    }
    public class main{
        public static void main(string[] args){
            Drawable d1 = new circle();
            Drawable d2 = new square();
            d1.draw();
            d2.draw();
        }
    }


// Multiple Interface Implementation (Solving the "Multiple Inheritance" Limitation)
    Recall : a class cannot extend multiple classes . But a class can implement multiple interfaces - this is how Java
    provides the flexibility of "multiple inheritance" safely , without the diamond problem , since interfaces
    (traditionally) don't provide conflicting implementations .

    interface Drawable {
        void draw();
    }

    interface Resizable {
        void resize();
    }
    class Circle implements Drawable,Resizable{
        @override
        public void draw(){
            sout("Drawing circle");
        }
        @override
        pubic void resize(){
            sout("Resizing circle");
        }
    }

// Abstract Class vs Interface - Comparison Table

                      Abstract Class                    Interface

      keyword        abstract class                     interface

      used with      extends                            implements

      Methods        can have both abstract And         Traditionally all abstract ( modern java allows some
                     concrete Methods                   exceptions , not needed at beginner level )

      variables      can have any type of variables     implicitly public static final (constants only)

      Multiple      A class can extend only one         A class can implement multiple interfaces
      Inheritance   abstract class

      Use when      classes share some common code      you just want to enforce what must be done ,
                    /state and an "is-a" relationship   across possibly unrelated classes


// The Super Keyword
    super refers to the immediate parent class . It's used for two main purposes :

    a) calling the parent's constructor :

        class Animal {
            String name ;
            Animal(String name){
                this.name = name ;
                sout("Animal constructor called");
            }
        }

        class Dog extends Animal{
            Dog(String name){
                super(name);
                sout("Dog constructor called");
            }
        }

        public class main{
            public static void main(String[] args){
                Dog d = new Dog("Tommy");
            }
        }

        // Important rule - Must be the very first statement in the subclass constructor . If you dont call it
           explicitly , java automatically inserts a call to the parent's no argument constructor .



    b) Calling the parent's method (when overridden);
        Class Animal{
            void sound() {
                System.out.println("Animals make sounds");
            }
        }
        class Dog extends Animal{
            void sound(){
                super.sound();
                System.out.println("Dog Barks");
            }
        }













